 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "BUS STOP. Route 57, Portree to Uig. Next bus: 'Soon'. The timetable was last updated: 'Recently'. A handwritten note underneath says the bus has gone for a wee coffee. It is not a lie, merely an optimistic forecast."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "PORT RÌGH · PORTREE. 'King's Port', said to be after King James V, who landed here in 1540 to calm the island clans down. The harbour houses were painted bright so fishermen could find their way home in the fog."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "RAASAY AND THE MINCH. Across the water is Raasay, and beyond is the Minch, home of the Blue Men, storm spirits who challenge sea captains to a rhyming contest. Finish the rhyme or lose your ship."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "AROS CENTRE. A theatre, a cinema and a gallery, with a view of the harbour. The Skye Gaelic Mod and the Highland Games are held in the village, and everyone, including the pipers, turns up in the rain."
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Flora MacDonald said goodbye to Prince Charles Edward Stuart at Portree in 1746, after helping him escape to Skye.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "portree_flora_macdonald",
   "title": "Flora's Farewell"
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "portree_battle_of_the_braes",
   "title": "Battle of the Braes",
   "text": "In 1882 crofters at the Braes, just south of Portree, refused to give up their grazing and faced police sent from Glasgow. The stand-off made headlines across Britain and helped bring about the Crofters Act of 1886, which finally gave crofters security on their land. Small place, long shadow."
  },
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "portree_sea_eagles",
   "title": "Sea Eagles",
   "text": "The white-tailed eagle, Britain's biggest bird of prey, with wings up to about 2.4 metres across, was wiped out in Britain by 1918. From 1975 young birds from Norway were released on the Isle of Rùm, and now they are back around Skye. People call them flying barn doors, and you will see why."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "portree_fairy_flag",
   "title": "The Fairy Flag",
   "text": "At Dunvegan Castle, seat of the MacLeods, hangs the Fairy Flag, a faded scrap of old silk. Legend says it was a gift from the fairies and will save the clan in battle when waved, but only three times. It has reputedly been used twice. Nobody is in a hurry to try the third."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "portree_johnson_and_boswell",
   "title": "Johnson and Boswell",
   "text": "In 1773 Samuel Johnson, the dictionary man, toured the Hebrides with his friend James Boswell, and on Skye they met Flora MacDonald herself. Johnson called hers a name that would be mentioned in history. At Kingsburgh he slept in the very bed the Prince had used, and seemed rather pleased about it."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "arts_sorley_maclean",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Somhairle MacGill-Eain",
   "text": "Sorley MacLean, Somhairle MacGill-Eain, was born in 1911 at \u00d2sgaig on Raasay, the island across the sound. His Gaelic poems, above all D\u00e0in do Eimhir in 1943 and 'Hallaig' in 1954, took the language's oldest music into the twentieth century. Hallaig is a township on Raasay cleared of its people in the 1850s; in the poem the woods fill again with the girls and the boys walking home. He was head teacher at Plockton for sixteen years and died in 1996. You can take the ferry from Sconser and walk to Hallaig. The trees are still there."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "arts_mairi_mhor",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "M\u00e0iri Mh\u00f2r nan \u00d2ran",
   "text": "Mary MacPherson, M\u00e0iri Mh\u00f2r nan \u00d2ran, 'Big Mary of the Songs', was born at Skeabost on Skye in 1821. Widowed in Inverness, she was jailed in 1872 on a theft charge she always denied, and said the injustice turned her to song. Her Gaelic songs of the 1880s, sung at the meetings of the land agitation, carried the crofters' case across the Highlands when the Braes men were fighting the police over grazing. She died in Portree in 1898. Her songs are still sung at ceilidhs, loudly, which is how she wrote them."
  }
 ],
